A **syntax-directed translation (SDT) scheme** is a CFG in which attributes are associated with the grammar symbols and semantic actions, enclosed within braces $\{\}$, are inserted into the right sides of the productions. The semantic actions are subroutines that the parser calls at appropriate times, which enables the translation.

An attribute value is made available when an action refers to it by:

1. Computing inherited attributes before they are used in actions.
2. Placing actions that compute synthesized attributes at the end of the production's right side, so that all needed attributes are available.

**Example**

```text
D -> T { L.type := T.type } L ;
T -> int  { T.type := int }
T -> real { T.type := real }
L -> { L1.type := L.type } L1 , id { enter(id.ptr, L.type); }
L -> id { enter(id.ptr, L.type); }
```

- In predictive (top-down) parsing, the actions are implemented within the production rules. Top-down parsers can easily call the translation routines right after recognizing the symbols.
- In contrast, bottom-up parsers handle attributes after reducing the symbols.

